+</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Enter the price and the rent. The calculator takes out the tax a rental pays in that town, the HOA dues, management, and repairs and empty months, and shows what is left. It opens on the Carolina Forest house in the sheet below.</p><div class="rnc" id="rnc"><div class="rnc-ex" role="group" aria-label="Load one of the three example homes"><span>Load an example:</span><button type="button" onclick="rncEx(0)" aria-pressed="true">Carolina Forest house</button><button type="button" onclick="rncEx(1)" aria-pressed="false">Longs house</button><button type="button" onclick="rncEx(2)" aria-pressed="false">Myrtle Beach condo</button></div><div class="rnc-grid"><div><fieldset><legend>The home</legend><div class="rnc-fields"><label class="rnc-f" for="rncZip">Area and ZIP code<select id="rncZip" onchange="rncZip()"><option value="29579" selected>Carolina Forest (29579)</option><option value="29568">Longs (29568)</option><option value="29577">Myrtle Beach (29577)</option><option value="29572">Myrtle Beach, north end (29572)</option><option value="29582">North Myrtle Beach (29582)</option><option value="29588">Socastee (29588)</option><option value="29566">Little River (29566)</option><option value="29576">Murrells Inlet and Garden City (29576)</option><option value="29575">Surfside Beach (29575)</option><option value="29526">Conway (29526)</option><option value="29527">Conway (29527)</option><option value="29569">Loris (29569)</option><option value="29511">Aynor (29511)</option></select></label><label class="rnc-f" for="rncBeds">Bedrooms<select id="rncBeds" onchange="rncZip()"><option value="2">2</option><option value="3" selected>3</option><option value="4">4 or more</option></select></label><label class="rnc-f" for="rncPrice">Asking price<span class="rnc-box"><span aria-hidden="true">$</span><input id="rncPrice" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="1000" value="390000" oninput="rncCalc()"></span></label><label class="rnc-f" for="rncRent">Rent a month<span class="rnc-box"><span aria-hidden="true">$</span><input id="rncRent" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="10" value="2230" oninput="rncCalc()"></span></label></div><p class="rnc-hint" id="rncRentNote"></p></fieldset><fieldset><legend>The costs</legend><div class="rnc-fields"><label class="rnc-f" for="rncTax">Where it is taxed<select id="rncTax" onchange="rncCalc()"><option value="201.0" selected>Outside city limits</option><option value="207.3">Murrells Inlet or Garden City, outside city limits</option><option value="214.2">Inside Surfside Beach</option><option value="221.2">Inside North Myrtle Beach</option><option value="254.6">Inside the City of Myrtle Beach</option><option value="272.5">Inside Conway</option><option value="282.2">Inside Loris</option></select></label><label class="rnc-f" for="rncDues">HOA dues a month<span class="rnc-box"><span aria-hidden="true">$</span><input id="rncDues" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="5" value="100" oninput="rncCalc()"></span></label><label class="rnc-f" for="rncIns">Insurance a year, your quote<span class="rnc-box"><span aria-hidden="true">$</span><input id="rncIns" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="50" oninput="rncCalc()"></span></label><label class="rnc-f" for="rncMgmt">Management<span class="rnc-box"><input id="rncMgmt" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="1" value="10" oninput="rncCalc()"><span aria-hidden="true">% of rent</span></span></label><label class="rnc-f" for="rncRep">Repairs and empty months<span class="rnc-box"><input id="rncRep" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="1" value="10" oninput="rncCalc()"><span aria-hidden="true">% of rent</span></span></label></div></fieldset><fieldset><legend>Your loan, if you use one</legend><div class="rnc-fields"><label class="rnc-f" for="rncPay">Loan payment a month, from your lender<span class="rnc-box"><span aria-hidden="true">$</span><input id="rncPay" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="10" oninput="rncCalc()"></span></label><label class="rnc-f" for="rncCash">Cash you put in, all in<span class="rnc-box"><span aria-hidden="true">$</span><input id="rncCash" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="1000" oninput="rncCalc()"></span></label></div></fieldset></div><div class="rnc-out" aria-live="polite"><p class="rnc-big"><span id="rncLeftL">Left before insurance and any loan</span><strong id="rncLeft">--</strong></p><p class="rnc-cap" id="rncCap"></p><dl class="rnc-lines"><div><dt>Rent a year</dt><dd id="rncRentY"></dd></div><div><dt>Property tax</dt><dd id="rncTaxY"></dd></div><div><dt>HOA dues</dt><dd id="rncDuesY"></dd></div><div><dt>Insurance</dt><dd id="rncInsY"></dd></div><div><dt>Management</dt><dd id="rncMgmtY"></dd></div><div><dt>Repairs and empty months</dt><dd id="rncRepY"></dd></div></dl><div class="rnc-tests"><div class="rnc-t"><b>1% rule price</b><strong id="rncOne">--</strong><span id="rncOneT"></span></div><div class="rnc-t"><b>Costs, of the rent</b><strong id="rncHalf">--</strong><span id="rncHalfT"></span></div><div class="rnc-t"><b>Coverage</b><strong id="rncCov">--</strong><span id="rncCovT"></span></div><div class="rnc-t"><b>Cash-on-cash</b><strong id="rncCoc">--</strong><span id="rncCocT"></span></div></div></div></div></div><script>var RNC_HUD={"29511":{"2":1320,"3":1630,"4":1900},"29526":{"2":1320,"3":1630,"4":1900},"29527":{"2":1320,"3":1630,"4":1900},"29566":{"2":1730,"3":2100,"4":2280},"29568":{"2":1870,"3":2270,"4":2460},"29569":{"2":1320,"3":1630,"4":1900},"29572":{"2":1500,"3":1820,"4":1980},"29575":{"2":1590,"3":1930,"4":2090},"29576":{"2":1720,"3":2080,"4":2420},"29577":{"2":1420,"3":1720,"4":1900},"29579":{"2":1840,"3":2230,"4":2420},"29582":{"2":1550,"3":1880,"4":2040},"29588":{"2":1540,"3":1870,"4":2030}};var RNC_EX=[{"zip":"29579","beds":"3","price":390000,"rent":2230,"mills":"201.0","dues":100},{"zip":"29568","beds":"3","price":320000,"rent":2270,"mills":"201.0","dues":100},{"zip":"29577","beds":"2","price":240000,"rent":1420,"mills":"254.6","dues":550}];function rncV(id){var e=document.getElementById(id);var v=e?parseFloat(e.value):NaN;return isFinite(v)&&v>0?v:0}function rncM(x){return (x<0?"-$":"$")+Math.round(Math.abs(x)).toLocaleString("en-US")}function rncS(id,t){var e=document.getElementById(id);if(e)e.textContent=t}function rncNote(){var z=document.getElementById("rncZip").value,b=document.getElementById("rncBeds").value,r=RNC_HUD[z]&&RNC_HUD[z][b];rncS("rncRentNote",r?"HUD's fair market rent for "+(b==="4"?"4":b)+" bedrooms in "+z+" is "+rncM(r)+" a month. Type your own rent if you know it.":"")}function rncZip(){var z=document.getElementById("rncZip").value,b=document.getElementById("rncBeds").value,r=RNC_HUD[z]&&RNC_HUD[z][b];if(r)document.getElementById("rncRent").value=r;rncPress(-1);rncNote();rncCalc()}function rncPress(i){var bs=document.querySelectorAll(".rnc-ex button");for(var k=0;k<bs.length;k++)bs[k].setAttribute("aria-pressed",k===i?"true":"false")}function rncEx(i){var x=RNC_EX[i];if(!x)return;document.getElementById("rncZip").value=x.zip;document.getElementById("rncBeds").value=x.beds;document.getElementById("rncPrice").value=x.price;document.getElementById("rncRent").value=x.rent;document.getElementById("rncTax").value=x.mills;document.getElementById("rncDues").value=x.dues;document.getElementById("rncMgmt").value=10;document.getElementById("rncRep").value=10;rncPress(i);rncNote();rncCalc()}function rncCalc(){var price=rncV("rncPrice"),rent=rncV("rncRent")*12,mills=parseFloat(document.getElementById("rncTax").value)||0,dues=rncV("rncDues")*12,ins=rncV("rncIns"),mg=rncV("rncMgmt")/100,rp=rncV("rncRep")/100,pay=rncV("rncPay"),cash=rncV("rncCash");var tax=price*0.06*mills/1000,m=rent*mg,r=rent*rp,costs=tax+dues+ins+m+r,left=rent-costs;rncS("rncRentY",rncM(rent));rncS("rncTaxY",rncM(tax));rncS("rncDuesY",rncM(dues));rncS("rncInsY",ins?rncM(ins):"Add your quote");rncS("rncMgmtY",rncM(m));rncS("rncRepY",rncM(r));rncS("rncLeftL",ins?"Left a year after costs, before any loan":"Left a year before insurance and any loan");rncS("rncLeft",rent?rncM(left):"--");rncS("rncCap",!price||!rent?"Enter a price and a rent.":(left/price*100).toFixed(1)+"% of the price"+(ins?", the cap rate.":". Add insurance to see the cap rate."));var need=rent/12*100;rncS("rncOne",rent?rncM(need):"--");rncS("rncOneT",!price||!rent?"":price<=need?"This price passes the 1% rule.":"This price is "+rncM(price-need)+" more than that.");rncS("rncHalf",rent?Math.round(costs/rent*100)+"%":"--");rncS("rncHalfT",rent?(ins?"of the rent goes to costs. The 50% rule expects about half.":"of the rent goes to costs before insurance. The 50% rule expects about half."):"");if(pay&&rent){var cov=(rent/12)/(pay+tax/12+ins/12+dues/12);rncS("rncCov",cov.toFixed(2));rncS("rncCovT",(cov>=1.25?"At or above":"Under")+" our 1.25 target"+(ins?".":". Add insurance for the full ratio."))}else{rncS("rncCov","--");rncS("rncCovT","Type your loan payment to see it.")}if(pay&&cash){var cf=left-pay*12;rncS("rncCoc",(cf/cash*100).toFixed(1)+"%");rncS("rncCocT",rncM(cf)+" a year after the loan"+(ins?".":", before insurance."))}else{rncS("rncCoc","--");rncS("rncCocT","Type your payment and the cash you put in.")}}document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",function(){rncNote();rncCalc()});</script><p class="rn-note">The rent fills in from HUD's fair market rent for the ZIP code and bedrooms. HUD figures it at the 40th percentile of rent plus utilities for a standard unit across the Myrtle Beach metro area, then adjusts it for each ZIP code. When the tenant pays the utilities, the rent you collect can be lower.</p><p class="rn-note">We allow 10 percent of the rent for management and 10 percent for repairs and empty months. These are allowances, not what managers charge. The tool quotes no loan and no rate: the coverage and cash-on-cash boxes use the payment and cash you type. For a full report on one address, our <a href="/invest/long-term-rental/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental analyzer</a> adds the permits filed nearby. For a nightly rental, the <a href="/invest/str-tools/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">short-term rental tools</a> estimate income by area, and the <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rules by city</a> say where it is allowed.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="sheet"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The sheet">What does the numbers sheet look like?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One page with every cost and where each number comes from. Here it is for the middle 3-bedroom house for sale in Carolina Forest, asking $390,000.</p><div class="rn-sheet" role="group" aria-label="The numbers sheet for the middle 3-bedroom house for sale in Carolina Forest"><div class="rn-sheet-h"><p>Numbers sheet: 3-bedroom house, Carolina Forest</p><span>Asking $390,000</span></div><div class="rn-line"><b>Rent</b><em>$26,760</em><small>$2,230 a month: HUD's fair market rent for 3 bedrooms in ZIP code 29579.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Property tax</b><em>- about $4,700</em><small>6 percent of the price, at 201.0 mills, the rate outside city limits, once the county resets the value after the sale.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>HOA dues</b><em>- $1,200</em><small>$100 a month: the middle HOA fee of 3-bedroom houses for sale in 29579.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Management</b><em>- $2,676</em><small>Our allowance: 10 percent of the rent.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Repairs and empty months</b><em>- $2,676</em><small>Our allowance: 10 percent of the rent.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Insurance</b><em>your quote</em><small>We can get you an insurance quote for the house before you make an offer.</small></div><div class="rn-total"><b>Left a year, before insurance and any loan</b><strong>about $15,500</strong><small>4.0 percent of the price</small></div><ul class="rn-chips"><li>The 1 percent rule would need a price of $223,000</li><li>Rent before any cost: 6.9 percent of the price a year</li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For an address you send, the rent comes from nearby rentals, the dues and rules from the association's papers, and the insurance from a quote for that house.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:760px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-run-the-numbers/carolina-forest-houses-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-run-the-numbers/carolina-forest-houses-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-run-the-numbers/carolina-forest-houses-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 760px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Rows of similar houses with gray roofs, short driveways and small fenced back yards along curving streets in Carolina Forest, seen from above" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>Streets of houses in The Parks of Carolina Forest, the area of the example sheet, from above.</figcaption></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The same sheet on three homes</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The two houses keep about 4 and 5 percent of their price a year before insurance and a loan. The condo keeps about 1.4 percent, because its dues take more than a third of the rent.</p><figure class="rn-rent"><span class="cap">Where each home's rent goes in a year</span><ul class="rn-key" aria-hidden="true"><li><i style="background:#1c2028"></i>Property tax</li><li><i style="background:#5c6b78"></i>HOA dues</li><li><i style="background:#9aa4ad"></i>Management</li><li><i style="background:#c9ced3"></i>Repairs and empty months</li><li><i style="background:#c4783a"></i>Left before insurance and a loan</li></ul><div role="img" aria-label="3-bedroom house, Carolina Forest: Property tax 18 percent, HOA dues 4 percent, Management 10 percent, Repairs and empty months 10 percent, Left before insurance and a loan 58 percent. 3-bedroom house, Longs: Property tax 14 percent, HOA dues 4 percent, Management 10 percent, Repairs and empty months 10 percent, Left before insurance and a loan 61 percent. 2-bedroom condo, inside the City of Myrtle Beach: Property tax 22 percent, HOA dues 39 percent, Management 10 percent, Repairs and empty months 10 percent, Left before insurance and a loan 20 percent."><div class="rn-home"><p><strong>3-bedroom house, Carolina Forest</strong>, asking $390,000, rent $26,760 a year</p><div class="rn-bar"><span style="width:17.57%;background:#1c2028;color:#fff">18%</span><span style="width:4.48%;background:#5c6b78;color:#fff"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#9aa4ad;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#c9ced3;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:57.94%;background:#c4783a;color:#1c2028">58%</span></div><p class="rn-sub">Left: about $15,500, 4.0 percent of the price.</p></div><div class="rn-home"><p><strong>3-bedroom house, Longs</strong>, asking $320,000, rent $27,240 a year</p><div class="rn-bar"><span style="width:14.17%;background:#1c2028;color:#fff">14%</span><span style="width:4.41%;background:#5c6b78;color:#fff"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#9aa4ad;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#c9ced3;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:61.43%;background:#c4783a;color:#1c2028">61%</span></div><p class="rn-sub">Left: about $16,700, 5.2 percent of the price.</p></div><div class="rn-home"><p><strong>2-bedroom condo, inside the City of Myrtle Beach</strong>, asking $240,000, rent $17,040 a year</p><div class="rn-bar"><span style="width:21.51%;background:#1c2028;color:#fff">22%</span><span style="width:38.73%;background:#5c6b78;color:#fff">39%</span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#9aa4ad;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#c9ced3;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:19.75%;background:#c4783a;color:#1c2028">20%</span></div><p class="rn-sub">Left: about $3,400, 1.4 percent of the price.</p></div></div><figcaption>The middle asking price and dues for each kind of home in its ZIP code, HUD's rent, the tax a rental pays there, and our 10 percent allowances. Not a value for any one home.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="one-percent-rule"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The 1% rule">Does the 1 percent rule work in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Almost never for a house. Of 2,975 houses with 3 or more bedrooms for sale in Horry County, 14 ask 100 times a month's rent or less, which is what the rule asks: a month's rent at 1 percent of the price.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A lower bar shows where the rent comes closest. 429 houses bring in 8 percent of their price a year in rent, before any cost, and 189 of them are in Longs. Longs also has 8 of the 14 that pass the 1 percent rule.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Houses for sale whose rent comes to 8 percent of the price a year"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Houses for sale whose rent comes to 8 percent of the price a year</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Area, houses that pass the 1% rule</span><span role="columnheader">houses at 8 percent or more</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Longs</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">8 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.563" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">189 of 336</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Loris</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">30 of 150</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Little River</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.187" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">34 of 182</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Carolina Forest</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">3 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.181" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">66 of 365</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Conway (29527)</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">2 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.123" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">24 of 195</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Galivants Ferry</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.095" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">6 of 63</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Socastee</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.095" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">25 of 264</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Conway (29526)</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.075" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">35 of 467</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Murrells Inlet and Garden City</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.028" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">6 of 216</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.017" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">3 of 181</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Surfside Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.012" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">2 of 161</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Myrtle Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.004" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">1 of 239</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach, north end</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">0 of 100</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Houses with 3 or more bedrooms for sale, each against HUD's fair market rent for its ZIP code and bedrooms. The bar is the share at 8 percent or more. Not shown: Aynor, 42 houses, and Nichols, 14 houses.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Condos pass on price, then the dues take it back. Of 1,785 condos with 2 or more bedrooms, listed at $50,000 or more, in the 10 ZIP codes we tested, 214 ask 100 times HUD's 2-bedroom rent or less. Counting the dues against the rent, 31 to 46 still pass.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">So a house here is judged on what is left after the costs, and on how prices move in its area. Our <a href="/invest/rental-returns/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental returns page</a> gives both, area by area, with our targets.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="tax"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The tax">Why is the tax on the listing not the tax you will pay?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Because the listing shows the seller's bill. After you buy, the county resets the value to about what you paid, and a rental pays the 6 percent rate and the school tax.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A home that is not its owner's legal residence, such as a house rented out year-round or a second home, is taxed on <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">6 percent of its value</a>. A legal residence is taxed on 4 percent and pays no school operating tax. A legal residence rented out more than 72 days in a calendar year loses the 4 percent rate, unless its owner is on active military duty under orders.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A rental pays all of the school operating tax: 109.1 mills in Horry County for 2026, the largest line on the bill everywhere but Loris.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read the county's deeds for 8,310 resales recorded January to September 2026, and the value each seller was taxed on. The middle home sold for 1.17 times that value, and a quarter sold at or below it. Half of them (4,223 of 8,310) carried the 4 percent legal-residence rate on the county's record for 2026.</p><figure class="rn-tax"><span class="cap">Your next tax bill against the seller's, outside city limits</span><div role="img" aria-label="For each $1,000 the seller paid: buying a home the seller lived in, your next bill is about $3,900; buying from another landlord, about $1,100."><div class="rn-tg"><p>Buying a home the seller lived in</p><div class="rn-tr"><span class="l">The seller's bill: <b>$1,000</b></span><span class="b" style="--f:0.250"></span></div><div class="rn-tr you"><span class="l">Your next bill: <b>about $3,900</b></span><span class="b" style="--f:0.980"></span></div></div><div class="rn-tg"><p>Buying from another landlord</p><div class="rn-tr"><span class="l">The seller's bill: <b>$1,000</b></span><span class="b" style="--f:0.250"></span></div><div class="rn-tr you"><span class="l">Your next bill: <b>about $1,100</b></span><span class="b" style="--f:0.275"></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The middle of 2,828 resales the seller's record showed at the 4 percent rate, and of 1,777 at 6 percent, recorded January to September 2026 outside city limits. Your bill is figured on the sale price. It leaves out any Homestead exemption the seller had, which would make the jump larger.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Buy one of those as a rental outside city limits, and your next bill is about 3.9 times the seller's. The middle half runs about 3.4 to 4.8 times. Buy from another landlord, and it is about 1.1 times. On the same house at the same value, a rental's bill is three to four times a home the owner lives in; the reset after a sale adds to that. In our experience, taxes stop more rental deals than insurance does.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">When the new bill starts</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you buy between January and September, your first October bill still uses the seller's value, and the next one uses the new value. Buy in October or later, and your first bill already uses the new value. <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/real-property-tax/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bills are mailed October 1</a> and are due by January 15.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When a home already taxed at 6 percent sells and stays at 6 percent, such as a rental bought from another landlord, 25 percent of its new value is exempt, but the taxed value cannot fall below the county's current market value. The owner or the owner's agent must notify the county assessor before January 31 of the first tax year it applies. We remind you before the deadline. Every town's rate is in our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax calculator</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="condo-dues"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Condo dues">What does a condo's HOA fee do to the numbers?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It takes a large share of the rent. More than half of condos for sale on the Grand Strand (1,552 of 2,828) list dues over $600 a month, and the middle condo's dues are between $600 and $700.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Against HUD's rent, the middle 2-bedroom condo's dues run from 23 percent of the rent in Carolina Forest to 48 percent at the north end of Myrtle Beach.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="The middle 2-bedroom condo's HOA dues, as a share of HUD's rent"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">The middle 2-bedroom condo's HOA dues, as a share of HUD's rent</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Area, dues and rent a month</span><span role="columnheader">dues, share of the rent</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Carolina Forest</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$425 dues on $1,840 rent</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.23" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">23% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Little River</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$500 dues on $1,730 rent</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.29" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">29% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Surfside Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$500 dues on $1,590 rent</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.31" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Murrells Inlet and Garden City</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$525 dues on $1,720 rent</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.31" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$550 dues on $1,420 rent</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.39" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">39% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$600 dues on $1,550 rent</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.39" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">39% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach, north end</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$725 dues on $1,500 rent</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.48" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">48% of the rent</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The middle monthly dues of 2-bedroom condos for sale in each ZIP code, against HUD's fair market rent for 2 bedrooms there.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The dues are in the 50 percent test below, and they are why the condo in the sheet above keeps so little. Our <a href="/invest/condo-buildings/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condo buildings page</a> lists dues building by building. A condo rented by the night has different numbers: our <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Airbnb income page</a> has them by area, and <a href="/invest/condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our condo guide</a> covers the building rules.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="ratios"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The ratios">What is a good cap rate, cash-on-cash return and coverage here?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our target for coverage is 1.25. Our targets for the other returns, and what each area returns, are on our <a href="/invest/rental-returns/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental returns page</a>. Each ratio below is in the calculator.</p><div class="rn-cards"><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span>Cap rate</h3><p>The rent left after costs in a year, divided by the price. No loan is in it, so it compares homes, not deals.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10.8 12.2 20 3M16.8 6.2l2.5 2.5M14.3 8.7l2 2"/><circle cx="7.5" cy="15.5" r="4.5"/></svg></span>Cash-on-cash return</h3><p>The cash left after the loan in a year, divided by the cash you put in. It changes with the loan, so it is yours to work out with your lender's payment.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span>Coverage (DSCR)</h3><p>The debt service coverage ratio: the rent divided by the monthly cost of the loan, taxes, insurance and dues. Our target is 1.25.</p><p class="f">A loan officer at our preferred lender starts writing DSCR loans at 0.75.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span>The 1 percent rule</h3><p>A month's rent should be at least 1 percent of the price. Here it fits almost no house, as the count above shows.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span>The 50 percent rule</h3><p>Investors' shorthand: the costs before the loan take about half the rent. Before insurance, they take about 42 percent on the Carolina Forest house, 39 percent in Longs and 80 percent on the Myrtle Beach condo.</p><p class="f">It fits the houses and fails the condo.</p></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="costs"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Every cost">What are the expenses on a rental property here?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Six come out of the rent on every long-term rental: the property tax, insurance, HOA dues, management, repairs and empty months. A nightly rental adds its own taxes and licenses.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Cost</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">What it is here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Property tax</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">6 percent of the value, plus the school tax, on a value reset to about the price after you buy. <a href="#tax" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Why the listing's tax is not yours</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Insurance</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A landlord policy costs <a href="https://www.iii.org/article/coverage-for-renting-out-your-home" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nationally about 25 percent more</a> than a homeowner policy; in our files, 15 to 20 percent more on the same house. Flood and wind are on our <a href="/invest/landlord-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">landlord insurance page</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>HOA dues</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The middle 3-bedroom house for sale pays $25 to $300 a month, by area. Condo dues are in <a href="#condo-dues" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the section above</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Management</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">We allow 10 percent of the rent. What managers charge here is on our <a href="/invest/property-management/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property management page</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Repairs and empty months</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">We allow 10 percent of the rent.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Nightly rental taxes and licenses</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Only on a short-term rental. <a href="/invest/accommodations-tax/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The accommodations tax</a>, by town.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Depreciation, a tax saving</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The building, not the land, is depreciated over <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">27.5 years</a> for federal tax. <a href="/invest/rental-depreciation/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">How it works</a>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-we-check"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What we check">What can a calculator not see?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The community's rules, how rents keep up with prices, and the seller's own numbers. These are three things we check for you before you offer.</p><div class="rn-cards"><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 21h18M5 21V9.5M19 21V9.5M5 9.5C7 6.8 9.4 5.5 12 5.5s5 1.3 7 4M12 5.5V21M8.5 7v14M15.5 7v14M5 15h14"/></svg></span>A rental ban</h3><p>Many communities ban renting the house at all. We filter those out when we search for you, and we read the HOA papers for rules that would block your plan.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span>Prices rose, the rent did not</h3><p>An agent now on our team helped buyers purchase about eight nearly identical townhouses in a new Little River community. Each rented for a little over $2,000.</p><p>Prices rose from $230,000 to $292,000 over about 18 months. The rents stayed flat, and the cash flow went away for later buyers.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span>The seller's cash flow is not yours</h3><p>An agent at Chapter3 sold a rented house for an owner of 15 years. His cash flow was over $1,200 a month. The next investor would make about $600, because of higher taxes and a bigger loan.</p><p>The agent marketed it to investors who accept a modest cash flow in an area where prices are rising. A cash buyer closed 13 days after it was listed.</p></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="run-form"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Send an address">What should you send us?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The address is enough. Investors ask us most to "send me properties with analytics to prove it", so that is what comes back: the sheet above for that home, with the rent from nearby rentals, the tax at the rate you will pay, the dues and the building's rules, and an insurance quote. It is free, and most come back the same day, evenings included.</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">The address, or the MLS number. Any home on the Grand Strand, listed with any brokerage, or one not for sale yet.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Your plan: long-term rental, short-term rental, mid-term rental or fix and flip. Not sure is fine.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">How you plan to pay, cash or a loan. We do not need your finances to run the home. <a href="/invest/cash-to-close/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">What you pay at closing</a>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Whether you will stay in it yourself. <a href="/invest/14-day-rule/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">How your own stays change the tax</a>.</li></ul><div id="rnWrap" style="max-width:680px;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:14px;padding:1.6rem;text-align:left;margin:1.4rem 0">
